  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268220/assets/9b05a420b0ddeaa7237407730ae587b99ef8f8fe164733bbe79cc5bc9c17d115.png)\n\nWhich of the polyhedra above are convex?",
  "worked_solution": "A polyhedron is convex if and only if the following condition holds:\n\n*If we take any two distinct points $A$ and $B$ that belong to the polyhedron, then all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ must also belong to the polyhedron.*  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our solids in turn.\n\n- Solids I and III are convex. Indeed, the above condition holds for any two points that belong to the solids.\n\n- Solid II is *not* convex. The above condition does not hold. As a counterexample, let's consider the points $A$ and $B,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268220/assets/2e50911a1da54ee0f618898479506628d8a72a710cb2138fb6531c84b4ba9559.png)\n\n Notice that some points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ lie outside the solid.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
